{
  "dom_id": "step-q347050",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/eda6639ced5b182d7a05017883cf15dfbc1ce4b11e4ad3161d0ed6f9d2ab982c.png)\n\nEstimate the area $A$ under the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+9x$ over the interval $[0,9]$ using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A\u2248$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+9x$ and let $A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA\u2248\\frac{1}{2}[f(a)+2(f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})+\u2026+f({x}_{n-1}))+f(b)]\u0394x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $\u0394x=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[0,9],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=9.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\n\u0394x=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{9-0}{3}=3.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA\u2248\\frac{1}{2}[f(0)+2(f(3)+f(6))+f(9)]\\cdot 3.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0$ | $18$ | $18$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA\u2248\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [0+2(18+18)+0]\\cdot 3=108\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave down* over the interval $[0,9],$ the trapezoids lie under the function, and therefore the approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
